  "notes": "The record that named bro-country — \"traditional country harmonies and a banjo backing with rock and roll stadium-show guitars and drums,\" with a rap-remix pipeline built in. The key set is a documented consequence of the production order, not a preference: \"flatter keys than the guitar-centric 90s lane because the tracks start from programmed beds, not open-position guitar,\" so B♭/A♭/C/D♭/E carry the weight, and the signature single is documented outright as \"B♭ major with the main chord pattern B♭–F–Gm7–E♭ = I–V–vi7–IV\" — that is the top family, with the seventh carried in `extensions` because the parser reads degrees only. The kit is the canonical bro-country template quoted in full: \"programmed claps and a sampled snare on 2&4 over a live kit, 16th hat/shaker loop, a short sampled sub-kick on 1 and the 'and-of-2,' and full stop-time drops before choruses. Verses frequently run on loop-plus-808 with no live kit at all.\" The 808 takes the `counter_riff` role, not `bassline`, because the entry places both layers explicitly: \"a genuine 808-style sub is standard here, short and tuned to the root, sitting under an overdriven electric bass; slides into the chorus downbeat appear on the rap-crossover cuts.\" Melody is \"paired lead voices in unison-then-thirds, rap-cadenced verses (high syllable density, on-the-16ths placement) opening to long-note singalong choruses.\" The arrangement carries a documented oddity: a section was \"added after the solo\" during recording, so an inserted post-solo section is in-grammar. ⚠ The non-documented per-track readings are [LOW-CONF]. ⚠ The INFERRED parameters are the 808 register and slide probability, the clap offsets and the counter ratio.",
